dentical results. Plan 015 runs spatial_bowl with LoRA-only as a control to see how CAR compares without the full training approach.\n\nNow I'm compiling the comprehensive YAML output with all 15 experiments and their predictions, focusing on the pending ones that are either running or planned. Looking at the experiment plan more carefully, I need to distinguish between the ones that are truly pending versus those that are cancelled or blocked. The user wants detailed predictions for the planned experiments that are actually viable, so I should focus on the ones without blockers — that means excluding plan_001 and plan_002, and covering the rest of the pending ones. I'm also pulling in the existing LoRA baseline data to use as reference points for my predictions. Now I'm looking at the performance metrics for the remaining tasks in that baseline run, then checking the corrected SDF results with lambda=0.005 across multiple seeds to verify the reported numbers. Looking at the corrected data for the other seeds with λ=0.01, I'm getting s1 and s2 values that average to about 52.8%, which matches what the paper reports. Now for the LoRA predictions, the first two seeds suggest a mean around 55.25%, and based on the pattern that LoRA consistently achieves roughly 55% CAR across different seeds due to the collision-free demonstration signal, I'm predicting the full result should land near CAR=55 with TSR around 6.\n\nFor the Phase 2 experiments with λ=0, since the SDF component didn't contribute meaningful safety improvements during the first 10K steps, removing it entirely for the remaining 20K steps means the model will continue optimizing purely on the collision avoidance signal without any additional safety constraints.\n\nFor spatial_bowl tasks, the geometry fundamentally differs from the object_pudding setup—the bowl presents a low, wide obstacle rather than tall bottles—which naturally results in lower collision avoidance rates, though the SDF training still produc